  "notes": "Vendor figures, read 2026-11-21 from https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/us-24-250w (HTTP 200, 638,959 B, rendered spec table): Port Layout \"1 GbE RJ45 24 (All PoE+) (1G/100M/10M) / 1G SFP 2 (1G)\"; \"PoE Ports PoE+ 24\"; \"Max. PoE Wattage per Port by PSE PoE 17W / PoE+ 34W\"; \"Max. PoE Output Up to PoE+\"; \"Total PoE Availability 250W\"; \"Max. Power Consumption 30W (Excluding PoE output)\". First-generation model - the SFP cages are 1G, not the 1G/10G the later Pro line has. Every metal port also listed only 1G/10M before this pass while the same page prints \"(1G/100M/10M)\"; the hundred-megabit rung is now on all 24. The 24 copper ports declared no poeOut before this pass, so engine/media.js derivePortHardware() filled the silence with the 802.3af default of 15.4 W and the faceplate printed \"PoE out 15.4 W\" on sockets the vendor rates at 34 W PoE+ - on a chassis whose 250 W aggregate then could not be described by its own per-port figure. 34 W and 250 W are both the vendor's; the first is a per-socket ceiling, the second the PSE allocation, and they are not meant to multiply.",
  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 250,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/us-24-250w",
    "note": "250 W, the vendor's own aggregate row: \"Max. PoE Output = Up to PoE+ / Total PoE Availability = 250W\" beside \"Port Layout = 1 GbE RJ45 24 (All PoE+) (1G/100M/10M)\", \"1G SFP = 2\" and \"PoE Ports = PoE+ 24\"; per-port maxima are printed on this page and are not the 802.3at defaults: \"Max. PoE Wattage per Port by PSE = PoE 17W / PoE+ 34W\" (read 2026-11-21). Max. Power Consumption 30W excluding PoE output. The figure the engine had been using came from the model name, which happens to agree here -- it is now traced to the document instead of to the SKU string."
  }
